  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/bd8a3ad5df3c4abe46a4b5612538092e549d1197f339494be7d263511d49b856.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(3x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(3x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(3x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/f6743a24307e16fbe5df98bf2ec3a34c5aa5673e2255cdfbd2aa65c9cc9e6aee.png)\n\n- Then, stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(3x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/a88eb4b9d68be3b491a00fc3ed9a738a6a2703f3658bbc131e8348c9498a0bfa.png)",
